  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continuing breezy and cool under mainly sunny to \npartly cloudy skies this afternoon in the wake of a cold front. \nSunday will remain on the breezy side with temperatures moderating \nsome compared to today. \n\nLow pressure and an associated cold front have moved offshore to our \neast but in the wake of this system there's a fairly strong pressure \ngradient due to high pressure centered near the center of the \ncountry. This will lead to it continuing breezy this afternoon with \nWNW winds generally around 15 gusting up to 25 mph. It should be \nmainly sunny for most of the region except up north in the southern \nPoconos into NW NJ where there will continue to be a bit more in the \nway of cloud cover. \n\nFor tonight, the winds remain elevated as the pressure gradient \npersists but they will diminish a bit compared to this afternoon, \nespecially in terms of gustiness. The winds along with some cloud \ncover moving through overnight will help keep temperatures from \nreally bottoming out. Generally expect lows in the low to mid 40s. \n\nFor Sunday, temperatures warm a few degrees compared to today with \nhighs mostly in the low 60s. Some morning clouds will give way to \nafternoon sun. Expect west winds generally around 10 to 15 gusting \nat times to around 20 mph due to a pressure gradient persisting \nbetween low pressure over eastern Canada and high pressure to our \nsouth and west.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A potentially strong coastal storm/nor'easter late \nnext week.\n\nA significant upper-level trough is forecast to evolve across the \neastern U.S. later next week. This should support a surface low that \ninitially tracks near the Ohio Valley, however a secondary low \nshould develop in the vicinity of the northern Mid-Atlantic. The \nmodel guidance overall is in decent agreement with the development \nof the strong upper-level trough, however there are some differences \non the handling of the embedded shortwaves and thus the surface low. \nThe timing and amplification of the trough will also have an impact \non the secondary surface low development and track. This will be \nimportant as it will determine where the main forcing for ascent \nbecomes established along with colder air on the west/northwest side \nof the developing coastal storm/nor'easter.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough would result in a \ncolder air mass moving in with time. This could be sufficiently cold \nenough for a rain/snow mix of even some snow across our far western \nto northwestern zones. This would have a greater chance of \noccurrence if the secondary low develops and takes over instead of \nthe initial low remaining primary and tracking over our area or to \nour west. Elsewhere, a decent amount of rain is possible. The amount \nof precipitation (rain and/or snow), and precipitation types for at \nleast portions of our area, will depend on the ultimate development \nand track of the secondary surface low and the associated thermal \nprofiles that evolve across our region. The coastal impacts, \nincluding the potential for stronger winds and also tidal flooding, \nare less certain because if the surface low tracks over our area \nthat would lessen the residence time of the (stronger) onshore flow. \nLots of uncertainty with the details this far out in time (Thursday \nnight through Friday night), therefore this time frame will continue \nto be monitored.",
